  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A warming trend begins today and peaks Friday, \nwith a cold air wedge building in for the weekend behind a cold\nfront passage.\n\n \nThe remnants of hurricane Polo in the eastern Pacific are\ncurrently merging with an upper level trough across the Desert\nSouthwest states. As this merging continues to progress eastward\ninto west Texas on Wednesday and Thursday, upper level ridging\nis expected to build over the southeast states, with the\nnorthern periphery likely across the Ohio valley and Mid-\nAtlantic states region. This will allow for increasing\ntemperatures through Friday before the arrival of a cold front\npushes into the region late on Friday. \n\nThe aforementioned upper level trough looks to stretched out\nover the upper level ridge over the Southeast, and merge with a\nshortwave trough that is progged to push east through the\nnorthern Great Lakes region by late Friday. Behind this cold\nfront, surface high pressure is expected to slide from the\nnorthern Great lakes region into the Northeast, which will\nbriefly lead to cold air wedging developing over VA/NC late\nSaturday and Sunday. This will dramatically cool temperatures\nareawide back to normal or just below normal for the weekend.\nThe possibility of a secondary shortwave trough and stronger\ncold front look to push into the region late Monday into\nTuesday, which looks to reinforce the cold air and cold air\nwedge across the region early next week. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The current period of dry and clear weather ends \nFriday into the weekend with increasing chances for rain. \n\nWith the approach of a cold front on Friday night, and moisture\nfrom the remnants from Polo overriding the front, the region\nlooks to be in store for a couple days of cool and rainy\nconditions through the weekend. Specifics on rainfall totals are\nstill uncertain at this time, as ensemble model guidance has\nshifted towards the surface high pressure and dry air aloft\ninhibiting rainfall production across the area. The cutoff line\nof dry vs moist air is quite evident and sharp, and the\nplacement of this line as the frontal boundary stalls will play\na great role in how much precipitation the area sees over the\nweekend. If this line remains across northern VA, much of the\narea will see beneficial rainfall; however, if this line of\ndrier air penetrates further south beyond the I-64 corridor,\nthen much of the area will miss out on any heavy rainfall.",
